  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nHigh pressure brings cooler temperatures through Friday. Fog risk\ntonight looks low as some wind will keep low-levels mixed enough.\nCould have a bit more patchy ground fog Saturday morning, though\nincreasing clouds from the south may restrict radiational cooling\nneeded for that. \n\nSpotty light rain for Halloween...Deep trough set up over the \neastern Pacific sends a southern stream trough toward the western \nGreat Lakes on Saturday. Not much surface reflection as the \ndeparting high holds. However, looking at soundings there is a\nmoist profile below 10kft with weak lift forecast, suggesting \nthat though chances of measurable rain (0.01\") will be under 50%, \ndrizzle or very light trace amounts of rain may occur beneath a \nmostly cloudy sky. Ensembles support this idea showing 30-60% \nchance of seeing at least a trace of rain, greatest over central\nWisconsin. But, not looking at any kind of washout for outdoor\nactivities. Even with the clouds and some rain, high temperatures\nwill still reach the mid to upper 50s which even though cooler \nthan lately, will be above average.\n\nStronger system with widespread rain early next week...After some\ndry weather returning Sunday into start of Monday, larger scale\ntroughing shifts across the plains and moves into the Great\nLakes-Ohio Valley. Surface low is well agreed upon from ensembles\nfrom ECMWF, GFS, and Canadian to track across northern Ohio\nValley, placing Wisconsin in regime that will bring periods of\nrain from late Monday through Wednesday as the system slowly\ncrosses. Peak of rain and gusty winds will be Tuesday, Election\nDay, but unsettled weather will be slow to unwind the rest of the\nweek behind the departing low. Chances for exceeding 0.50 inch\nrain Monday Night through Tuesday are up to 70%, while chances for\nexceeding 1.00 inch are up to 40-60%. Higher end projections take\nrainfall to 1.5 to 2.0 inches. Certainly looks like a soaking \nrain at times. Dynamics may be strong enough for some embedded \nthunder as the initial warm air advection rain arrives on Monday \nnight.\n\nTemperatures will remain above normal the next week, though a bit\nof a cooldown (temperatures returning to around normal) occurs \nlate next week after the low pressure system passes by. Available\nlong range guidance points to above normal temperatures through \nmost of November. Official forecast for meteorological winter (Dec\nthrough Feb) also favors above normal temperatures which is right\nin line with the ongoing very strong El Nino.",
